  "status": "published",
  "title": "Adirondack Chair",
  "summary": "The classic slanted-back chair, from a full-size template. Curved slats, stainless screws.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 10,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Trace the template onto the boards: the two back legs (stringers) and the arms from the 2x6 and 1x6 stock, the seat and back slats from the 1x4 and 1x6 stock. Lay the parts out so no knot lands on a curve or a screw line.",
    "Cut the curves with the jigsaw, staying a hair outside the line, and make the straight crosscuts against a speed square. Sand every edge down to the line.",
    "Build the seat frame: screw the front apron across the front ends of the two stringers, then measure both diagonals. They must match within 1/8 in before you go on.",
    "Bolt the front legs to the outside of the stringers with the carriage bolts, with the legs plumb and the stringers at the height the template shows.",
    "Screw the seat slats to the stringers with 1/4 in spacers between them. Pre-drill and countersink every hole so the cedar does not split, and use only stainless screws: cedar stains black around plain steel.",
    "Fit the lower and upper back supports, then screw on the back slats, fanned out from the centre slat. Trace the top curve with a string compass and cut it in place with the jigsaw.",
    "Glue and screw the arm braces to the front legs, set the arms level on top, and screw them down into the legs and the upper back support.",
    "Sand to 180 grit, round over every edge a hand or arm will touch, and brush on two coats of timber oil a day apart."
  ],
